    "lawCode": "Amd §§4174 & 4179, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A3686A\n\nTITLE  OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nestablishing no fee shall be charged for certain  requests  for  birth\ncertificates\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL: To allow the Department of Correctional Services,\nlocal  correctional  facilities  and  juvenile detention facilities to\nrequest birth certificates for inmates or juveniles in anticipation of\nrelease.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:   Section 1 of  the  bill  amends  the\npublic health law to provide that the state department of health shall\nnot  charge  a  fee  when  a  birth  certificate  is  requested by the\ndepartment of correctional services, a local correctional facility, or\na  juvenile  detention  facility  for  an  inmate   or   juvenile   in\nanticipation of release.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends the public health law to provide that the\nCity  of  New  York shall not charge a fee when a birth certificate is\nrequested  by  the  department  of  correctional  services,  a   local\ncorrectional  facility, or a juvenile detention facility for an inmate\nor juvenile in anticipation release.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: Persons being released  from  incarceration  face  many\nbarriers  to  a  successful  reentry to the community. One significant\nbarrier  is  the  inability  to  obtain  proper  identification  (i.e.\ndriver's    license,    social   security   card).   Securing   proper\nidentification  is  vital  to  an  inmate's  ability  to  find  stable\nemployment,  housing and secure benefits. Providing each-person with a\nbirth certificate upon release will allow them to more  easily  obtain\nthe necessary identification.\n\nWhile  the  New  York  State  Department of Health has been willing to\nprovide  birth  certificates  free  of   charge,   there   have   been\ncircumstances  in which the City of New York, which is responsible for\nall birth certificates of persons born in the City, has been unwilling\nto waive the fee when a birth certificate is requested  by  a  prison,\njail  or  juvenile  detention  facility.  Inmates  and  juveniles  are\ngenerally unable to pay the fee and the facilities  do  not  have  the\nfunds  to  pay  for  each birth certificate. Therefore, this bill will\nprovide by law that birth certificates  be  provided  to  DOCS,  local\ncorrectional  facilities  and  juvenile  detention  facilities free of\ncharge.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE & LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:  There is  no  cost\nto  the  state  because  the department of health is already supplying\nbirth certificates to  facilities  free  of  charge  as  a  matter  of\npractice.    There  may be some cost to the City of New York but it is\nminimal because the City has  been  providing  birth  certificates  to\nprisons  and jails but has stamped such certificates \"not for personal\nuse.\" Therefore, the certificates are already being produced and  sent\nto  correctional  agencies.  This bill would simply require that these\ncertificates for certified.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: Passed Assembly in 2005 and 2009.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect on the thirtieth day  after\nit shall have become a law.\n\n",
